            "diagnosis_primary.0",
            "diagnosis_primary.1"
          ],
          "text": "Thank you for referring this 10-month-old male who presented with a 3-day history of coryza, worsening cough, noisy breathing, poor feeding and reduced wet nappies, and was diagnosed with RSV-positive bronchiolitis.",
          "unverified_fact_ids": []
        },
        {
          "section_id": "sections.12",
          "part": "part_b",
          "heading": "admission_summary",
          "fact_ids": [
            "key_investigations.0",
            "key_investigations.1",
            "key_investigations.2",
            "key_investigations.3",
            "treatment.0",
            "treatment.1",
            "treatment.2",
            "treatment.3",
            "treatment.4"
          ],
          "text": "On admission he had a respiratory rate of 58, temperature 37.6, mild-moderate subcostal recession, widespread fine creps and wheeze, and oxygen saturations of 90% on room air. NPA confirmed RSV. He was managed with supportive care and supplemental oxygen to maintain saturations ≥90%, and nasogastric feeds were commenced (IV fluids available as contingency). Antibiotics were not given given the viral aetiology, and salbutamol was not given as it is not indicated in bronchiolitis. By Day 2 his saturations were 93–95% on room air, respiratory rate had improved to 44, recession was mild only, and he was tolerating approximately 75% oral feeds; the nasogastric tube was removed accordingly.",
          "unverified_fact_ids": []
        },
        {
          "section_id": "sections.13",
          "part": "part_b",
